# A FIXED DEFECT, named after it (Go's `test/fixedbugs`).
#
# SYMPTOM (2026-08-11, M6's closure-list audit, first program): `map(xs, to_str)`
# checked clean at exit 0 and then failed to compile as C —
#
#   typedef HeroValue (*h_m_fn0)(int64_t);
#   error: type specifier missing, defaults to 'int'
#
# — reported as exit 2, which CLAUDE.md §7 reserves for the compiler being wrong.
#
# CAUSE: `types/exprs.rs` answered `error_ty()` for a built-in used as a value
# **with no diagnostic**, and its comment said the case was "only reachable as a
# callee". That was false: `f = to_str` and `map(xs, to_str)` both reach it. An
# error type with nothing reported is poison — every other `error_ty()` in this
# checker means "a mistake was already reported", and this one meant nothing.
#
# FIX: say so. The rule already existed and only the message was missing — spec
# line 89 says *top-level functions* are values, and a built-in is not one.
# Several of them could not be, whatever the spec said: `print` takes any number
# of arguments of four types and `len` works on three, which is exactly why they
# are built-ins rather than declarations.
#
# The fix note names the wrapper, because a reader would otherwise have to invent
# its shape — and the llm-ergonomist predicted at panel 029 that ≥80% of
# higher-order attempts would write one. It was right, and the compiler now says
# so instead of leaving it to be discovered.
# Bulk regression case.

function main()
    f = to_str          #~ builtin_as_value
    print(f(1))
    xs = [1, 2]
    print(len(map(xs, len)))   #~ builtin_as_value
